Hi,

While trying to isolate the problem I came across a setting that solved 
my issue. Passing maxcpus=1 kernel parameter seemed to do the trick. 
This however isn't a permanent solution as this forces the computer to 
only use one cpu, so I kept looking.

I then noticed something while trying to read early printk messages: the 
clk subsystem disables all unused clocksources, but right after this the 
tsc clocksource is selected. The output looks like this

...
port serial18250:0.0: hash matches
acpi_cpufreq: overriding BIOS provided _PSD data
RAS: Correctable Errors collector initialized
clk: Disabling unused clocks
Freeing unused decrypted memory: 2036K
Freeing unused kernel image (initmem) memory: 4848K
tsc: Refined TSC clocksource calibration: 2611.647
clocksource: tsc: freq: 2611647000 Hz, mask: Oxffffffffffffffff 
max_cycles: 0x25a536db2f0, max_idle_ns: 440795310479 ns
clocksource: Switched to clocksource tsc

after which it hangs, then outputs the stacktrace described previously 
at 60 second intervals. I thought that perhaps something goes wrong with 
the clocksource selection and somehow the kernel tries to select a 
disabled clocksource. I removed Power Management Timer Support 
(CONFIG_X86_PM_TIMER=n), along with other latency related configs like 
CPU Frequency Scaling (CONFIG_CPU_FREQ=n) from the kernel configuration 
and tried adding "clocksource=tsc tsc=reliable" kernel boot parameters 
to avoid clocksource switching, and that combination booted the machine 
correctly!

Is this a bug in the logic? I'm not sure how to prove that this is a 
problem with the code or my machine(s) are just too old. I've only been 
able to test this on one machine and will report results for the others. 
Attached is the kernel configuration that worked.

-Ivan

